cell.lisp is the plugin's rescue half, rewritten in Common Lisp against the
Nodecode cell contract. The original's transform_tool_result hook becomes one
hook on the core's :TOOL point: a tool result over max_result_chars is
written to a content-addressed store (the first 12 hex characters of its
SHA-256, as the original's handle is) and the model reads a head-and-tail
excerpt plus a handle line. rescuer_fetch keeps the same name, the same
modes it could carry in Common Lisp - outline, search, grep, range, stat,
full - and the same answer shapes; /rescuer is the original's /rescuer status
command; the tests prove the spill, every mode and the lifecycle, offline.

Left out, and why: pass-by-reference (the tla:<id> token expanded inside a
downstream tool's arguments) needs the host's tool-request middleware, which
this contract does not expose, so a handle is read with rescuer_fetch only.
Also left out: semantic search through sentence-transformers (search here
ranks by the words a line shares with the query, so the cell carries no
Python and no model download), the optional Fernet encryption of credential
tier blobs and its key-rotation command, the hot-blob pinning and size-budget
sweeps (a sweep here drops blobs older than ttl_hours at start), the chained
version grammar (<id>@N) and the expansion audit ledger.
